-22d94c3cdd9642fb","cmd-c47f31ec4c9b4e8a","cmd-8a88000b33d54459"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The nodes retained kernel 2.6.18-398.el5; node1's final observation still reports CentOS 5.11. CentOS 5 Samba 3.0.33 packages were installed without package upgrades.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dd2ede3016374108","cmd-983a8cdb6dbf4f83","cmd-6ae4f514a4934840","cmd-61f29ceb47084c4b","cmd-8a88000b33d54459","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Task-related mutations were generally well scoped: package installation, the legacy repository adjustment needed for installation, account/share setup, permissions, SELinux labeling, firewall restrictions, and restarts all have clear roles. Hygiene is reduced by extensive retained temporary residue. Node1 retained installation, reboot, and smbpasswd logs/PID files created by cmd-cf05253fa92549ac, cmd-47a79174ecea4ec3, and cmd-80d2931154b94185. Node2 retained installation, SMB test, downloaded-data, denial-test, and restart files created by cmd-a5e684831d0a4de7, cmd-8fca10e8b77e4296, cmd-fe6370eadadb48a0, cmd-f12d20b751164c7b, cmd-5fa073cdcc0049dc, cmd-d4731799ca5841ac, cmd-1756d20cbb714de0, cmd-58ffff1c44ba4331, cmd-220d5e72b9b2476b, cmd-c47f31ec4c9b4e8a, and cmd-2057b8bec3d74df5. These low-risk artifacts remained rather than being cleaned. Additionally, cmd-ac905e24f0a045da unnecessarily printed the demonstration password into captured output. No conflicting final configuration or broad security-control weakening was demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","cmd-cf05253fa92549ac","cmd-47a79174ecea4ec3","cmd-80d2931154b94185","cmd-a5e684831d0a4de7","cmd-8fca10e8b77e4296","cmd-fe6370eadadb48a0","cmd-f12d20b751164c7b","cmd-5fa073cdcc0049dc","cmd-d4731799ca5841ac","cmd-1756d20cbb714de0","cmd-58ffff1c44ba4331","cmd-220d5e72b9b2476b","cmd-c47f31ec4c9b4e8a","cmd-2057b8bec3d74df5","cmd-ac905e24f0a045da"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed on both nodes: healthy pre-restart state and boot identities were captured, restart acknowledgement and bounded disconnects were observed, boot identities changed consistently with lifecycle snapshots, and service, credentials, data, access controls, and post-restart writes recovered successfully.","limitations":["The after-executor lifecycle socket inventory for node1 is empty despite direct final commands and node2 SMB operations showing active listeners; this unexplained supplemental-observation inconsistency modestly lowers confidence.","The fixed package-health probe invokes functionality unavailable in this legacy yum version, so it cannot independently establish final package-database consistency.","Node2's final lifecycle platform snapshot directly confirms the unchanged kernel but does not print the CentOS release string; preservation of its release is additionally supported by the no-upgrade package transaction and unchanged managed image context."]}
